{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DzMRMB4GoQ1uMb2QAMfucm","type":"tool_result","content":"2:## *\"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"*\n10:## Attention Budget\n42:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: Outline Open Question 11 flagged this epigraph as an option, not a recommendation — it reuses Ch 14's closing quote verbatim as a deliberate callback binding the two Part IV delivery chapters. The structural contract prefers outside voices for epigraphs, and a reader met this line eight pages ago, which costs arousal. Author's call: keep the callback, or substitute a practitioner quote about intent versus outcome. -->\n46:## 🧭 Soundings\n99:## Why This Chapter Matters\n129:## What You'll Learn\n144:## ⚪ §1 — Twelve Factors, and the Ones Kubernetes Already Solved\n195:### Factor III — Config\n209:### Factors VI and VIII — Processes and Concurrency\n219:### Factor IX — Disposability\n229:### Factor XI — Logs\n245:## 🔵 §2 — Ways to Replace What's Running\n257:### The umbrella: progressive delivery\n263:### The four, and what each costs\n319:### One term this book will not teach you\n331:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 1: The Application, and the Shapes of a Release\n384:## 🔵 §3 — Push, or Pull\n392:### The three letters, expanded\n404:### The architectural question\n460:### GitOps\n512:### One thing GitOps does not do\n530:## 🔵 §4 — An Agent That Watches a Repository\n550:### The object model\n570:### Where the manifests come from\n580:### What it tracks\n594:### Synced, and OutOfSync\n606:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: argocd-diffing-outofsync-2026-08-31 quotes the claim but its own capture note says the page's enumerated causes (unknown fields, pruning disabled, mutating controllers/webhooks, Helm template functions, HPA metric reordering) were returned as summary rather than quotation and must not be attributed to that snapshot. The claim above is therefore stated without its causes, which weakens it. A fetch of the full diffing page would let §4 name at least one concrete cause. -->\n643:### Doing something about it\n651:[Omitted long matching line]\n655:### Rollback, for the third time\n673:### The agent's own identity\n705:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 2: Push, Pull, and the Agent\n766:## 🟡 §5 — Ordering the Sync\n778:### Phases\n795:### Waves\n849:## 🔵 §6 — The Other Agent, and More Than One Cluster\n879:[Omitted long matching line]\n895:Flux's answer is per-cluster: each cluster runs its own Flux, bootstrapped into its own repository or its own path within a shared one, each pulling independently. No cluster holds credentials to another.\n903:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 3: Ordering, and the Other Agent\n954:## ☀️ §7 — The Control Loop, Pointed at a Repository\n1013:### The four principles, re-read\n1033:### Why the chapter is called what it is\n1045:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1062:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n1093:## Practice Questions\n1265:[Omitted long matching line]\n1273:## Chapter Summary\n1302:## The Voyage Ahead"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"079f780d-5ce5-4a84-a0f4-ce15c4c5192e","uuid":"01ba63aa-4bfb-4b4d-be61-610f0122e321","timestamp":"2026-08-31T10:33:43.506Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"2:## *\"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"*\n10:## Attention Budget\n42:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: Outline Open Question 11 flagged this epigraph as an option, not a recommendation — it reuses Ch 14's closing quote verbatim as a deliberate callback binding the two Part IV delivery chapters. The structural contract prefers outside voices for epigraphs, and a reader met this line eight pages ago, which costs arousal. Author's call: keep the callback, or substitute a practitioner quote about intent versus outcome. -->\n46:## 🧭 Soundings\n99:## Why This Chapter Matters\n129:## What You'll Learn\n144:## ⚪ §1 — Twelve Factors, and the Ones Kubernetes Already Solved\n195:### Factor III — Config\n209:### Factors VI and VIII — Processes and Concurrency\n219:### Factor IX — Disposability\n229:### Factor XI — Logs\n245:## 🔵 §2 — Ways to Replace What's Running\n257:### The umbrella: progressive delivery\n263:### The four, and what each costs\n319:### One term this book will not teach you\n331:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 1: The Application, and the Shapes of a Release\n384:## 🔵 §3 — Push, or Pull\n392:### The three letters, expanded\n404:### The architectural question\n460:### GitOps\n512:### One thing GitOps does not do\n530:## 🔵 §4 — An Agent That Watches a Repository\n550:### The object model\n570:### Where the manifests come from\n580:### What it tracks\n594:### Synced, and OutOfSync\n606:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: argocd-diffing-outofsync-2026-08-31 quotes the claim but its own capture note says the page's enumerated causes (unknown fields, pruning disabled, mutating controllers/webhooks, Helm template functions, HPA metric reordering) were returned as summary rather than quotation and must not be attributed to that snapshot. The claim above is therefore stated without its causes, which weakens it. A fetch of the full diffing page would let §4 name at least one concrete cause. -->\n643:### Doing something about it\n651:[Omitted long matching line]\n655:### Rollback, for the third time\n673:### The agent's own identity\n705:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 2: Push, Pull, and the Agent\n766:## 🟡 §5 — Ordering the Sync\n778:### Phases\n795:### Waves\n849:## 🔵 §6 — The Other Agent, and More Than One Cluster\n879:[Omitted long matching line]\n895:Flux's answer is per-cluster: each cluster runs its own Flux, bootstrapped into its own repository or its own path within a shared one, each pulling independently. No cluster holds credentials to another.\n903:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings 3: Ordering, and the Other Agent\n954:## ☀️ §7 — The Control Loop, Pointed at a Repository\n1013:### The four principles, re-read\n1033:### Why the chapter is called what it is\n1045:## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1062:## Exam Alert! 🚨\n1093:## Practi